+# Perf history
+
+## UserPromptSubmit read path
+
+Searched the repo and full git log (`crates/muninn-core/src/recall.rs`,
+`crates/muninn-bench/src/main.rs`, `research/00-evidence-log.md`, all commit
+messages and diffs) for a measured full-recall p95 of the `UserPromptSubmit`
+hook on the adversarial synthetic 30-word vocabulary, before and after a
+term-selection fix.
+
+**No such measurement exists in this repository or its git history.**
+
+What does exist, for context:
+
+- The 30-word adversarial vocabulary is real: `crates/muninn-bench/src/main.rs`
+  builds a Zipf-distributed synthetic store with "30 frequent domain words
+  plus 2 000 rare identifiers" specifically so posting lists resemble real
+  code discussion.
+- The read path's term selection is IDF top-8 via `fts5vocab`, with terms
+  present in more than 1/3 of the store dropped (`select_terms` in
+  `crates/muninn-core/src/recall.rs`). This logic was written once, in commit
+  `0dcfc84`, and has not been changed since — there is no "before" and "after"
+  commit pair for it.
+- `crates/muninn-bench` does define a perf contract named "hook
+  UserPromptSubmit full p95" with a 10 ms limit, but no historical run output
+  recording a before/after pair for the adversarial vocabulary case was found.
+- A `10.27 ms` figure does appear in the repo, but only as fixture data in a
+  `recall.rs` unit test (`passage_prefers_the_window_with_the_terms`) — it is
+  test-fixture text, not a measurement, and is unrelated to term selection.
+
+If this number was measured, it was not committed to this repository or
+recorded in any commit message.
